+# Eager Ascend causal convolution
+
+`tokenspeed_kernel.ops.conv` conditionally imports `conv.ascend` on NPU.
+Its two registered leaves, `ascend_causal_conv1d_prefill` and
+`ascend_causal_conv1d_update`, preserve the existing public convolution ABI.
+Registration requires vendor `ascend`, dense FP16/BF16/FP32 input, width
+2/3/4/5, and channel-major or width-major state. Update advertises **tree=False
+only**. Priority is `PORTABLE + 1`: this device-local correctness implementation
+wins over the ungated portable Triton leaves, without claiming optimized status.
+CPU callers cannot enter these registry adapters. The independent
+`tokenspeed_kernel_npu.ops.conv` helpers also accept CPU tensors for tests;
+they do not implement automatic accelerator-to-CPU fallback.
+
+## Math and state ABI
+
+For each channel, the pool stores the last `W-1` **raw input values**, oldest
+first. Tap `weight[:, W-1]` multiplies the current token. Products, sums, bias
+and optional SiLU are evaluated in FP32 on the input device, then output is
+cast to the input dtype. `None`/`False` disables activation;
+`"silu"`/`"swish"`/`True` enables it. There is no residual term. Weight and bias
+may use any supported floating dtype on the input device; state must use the
+input dtype. No activation, state, weight or bias is copied to the host.
+
+State shapes are exactly `[slots,C,W-1]` (channel-major) or `[slots,W-1,C]`
+(width-major), including noncontiguous channel-sliced/stepped views. Layout is
+inferred by shape exactly as the public wrapper does: **if `C == W-1`, the
+ambiguous square shape is interpreted as width-major**, irrespective of strides.
+Strided input, taps, bias, routing indices and state are supported; mutable
+views must be nonoverlapping, and must not overlap other arguments. Broadcast
+strides on mutable tensors are rejected; arbitrary overlapping `as_strided`
+views are outside the contract.
+
+This consumes caller-owned state slots; it introduces no backend-private
+request state. The scheduler/LCM cache retains ownership of checkpoints and
+live blocks, as described in `docs/design/cache-concepts.md`.
+
+## Supported calls and mutation
+
+| Contract | Prefill | Greedy update |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| Input/output | Packed `[C,T]`; separate output, input unchanged | `[B,C]` or `[B,C,1]`; **overwrites and returns the same `x` object** |
+| Routing | `cache_indices: [B]`, or implicit `arange(B)` | `conv_state_indices: [B]`, or implicit `arange(B)` |
+| Output state | Same slots as prefill input; runtime prepares those live slots before the call | `output_state_indices: [B,1]`, or source slots in place |
+| History/reset | `has_initial_state: bool[B]`; True consumes nonzero history, False/omitted uses zeros | Always consumes the indexed source window; reset/zero it before calling if needed |
+| Negative source | Skip sequence, return zeros for its token range, do not touch state | Skip request, leave its `x` row unchanged, do not write any destination |
+| Negative destination | Not a separate argument | Compute output normally for a live source, suppress state write |
+| Empty work | Zero-length sequences leave state unchanged, including a reset flag; empty batches supported | `[B,C,0]` and zero batch are no-ops |
+
+All negative routing indices are holes, never Python wraparound indices.
+`pad_slot_id` must be a negative integer (including custom sentinels); `None`
+and nonnegative sentinels are rejected. Metadata indices are int32/int64 and
+may reside on CPU or the input device. Every nonnegative index is bounds-checked
+before any mutation, even on padded/empty rows. Multiple active requests may
+read the same source, but must not write the same destination. Empty or
+negative-source requests do not count as writers.
+
+**All active source windows are cloned before the first write.** Thus crossed
+maps such as `sources=[1,0], destinations=[[0],[1]]`, shared immutable prefix
+sources with distinct destinations, and in-place updates remain isolated.
+Raw update inputs are also snapshotted before `x` is overwritten.
+
+Prefill requires the original `prefill_metadata`. The adapter forwards every
+field to the independent implementation. Boundaries must be monotone from 0
+to at most `T`; unassigned trailing token capacity produces zeros. Each
+`(batch, chunk_offset)` implied by `block_m` must occur exactly once. Inactive capacity-program entries have batch index -1. Optional
+`cu_seqlens_cpu` must match device boundaries. All schedule fields are checked;
+none is silently dropped. Schedule tensors must be on the input device.
+After validation, sequence-wise math is independent of the Triton chunk size.
+`validate_data=True` and `False` both run full validation.
+
+## Explicitly unsupported
+
+- Tree parents, `num_accepted_tokens`, `intermediate_conv_window`, and
+  `cache_seqlens` (ring cursors), even if supplied as empty/zero tensors.
+- Multi-token `[B,C,L]` update with `L > 1`, speculative MTP/chain verify,
+  per-token speculative destinations, extended/ring state storage, and
+  missing state pools. Ordinary prefill can be chunked at any token boundary.
+- Other activations, widths/dtypes outside the stated domain, malformed or
+  incomplete schedules, out-of-bounds slots and duplicate active destinations.
+- Graph capture/replay, autograd, and concurrent calls mutating the same pool.
+
+Unsupported options passed directly to either Ascend update leaf/helper fail
+before mutation. **Registry `tree=True` requests exclude the Ascend leaf but
+may still select an existing Triton leaf**; this change does not disable other
+providers or alter runtime policy. An Ascend deployment must select plain eager
+greedy execution with speculation disabled. Runtime graph selection,
+checkpoint preparation, other convolution families (Inkling ring/DFlash2), and
+GDN/SSM operators remain separate integration responsibilities.
+
+## Synchronization and verification
+
+Python `.tolist()` reads only routing flags, indices, sequence boundaries and
+program maps. On NPU this intentionally synchronizes metadata at each call
+(and therefore each layer). Device-local explicit tap multiplies avoid vendor
+convolution-library requirements, but allocate FP32 temporaries and issue
+several eager operations per request. This is bounded correctness coverage,
+not a serving-performance implementation or a graph-compatible path.
+
+With PyTorch/pytest and this package importable:
+
+```bash
+PYTHONPATH=tokenspeed-kernel-npu/python python -m pytest tokenspeed-kernel-npu/test/test_causal_conv.py -k cpu
+PYTHONPATH=tokenspeed-kernel-npu/python:tokenspeed-kernel/python python -m pytest tokenspeed-kernel-npu/test/test_causal_conv.py -k npu
+```
+
+The parameterized fixture uses actual CPU/NPU devices; NPU cases skip only if
+`torch_npu` or hardware is absent. Direct tests compare against an independent
+CPU float64 grouped `F.conv1d` oracle, exercise full/chunk/one-step parity,
+nonzero history/reset, empty sequences, both state layouts, crossed and shared
+sources, independent destinations, negative padding, strided-view guards,
+activation/bias combinations and nonmutating validation failures. An NPU-only
+test calls the actual public registry entry points with upstream metadata.
+Public integration still requires the repository's normal Triton/import and
+NPU dependencies: this bounded change does not remove existing convolution
+package imports. Without local PyTorch, only syntax validation is possible;
+numerical and real-NPU execution must be run in the integration environment.
