+# Ascend GDN: eager correctness implementation
+
+`tokenspeed_kernel.ops.attention.gdn` registers
+`ascend_gdn_chunk_prefill` and `ascend_gdn_decode_step` with solution `ascend`.
+The shared package only adapts the registry and structured prefill result;
+all arithmetic lives in `tokenspeed_kernel_npu.ops.gdn`. This is a slow,
+single-device, inference-only implementation intended to establish the ABI,
+not a fused performance kernel or an end-to-end runtime enablement.
+
+## Arithmetic and layouts
+
+Q/K have `H` heads and values have `HV` heads, with `HV >= H` and `HV % H == 0`.
+Value head `hv` uses Q/K head `hv // (HV // H)`. Q/K/V share FP16, BF16 or FP32
+dtype; all recurrent states, products, gates and reductions are FP32. For
+K-last state `S[HV,V,K]`, each token computes:
+
+```text
+S_decay = exp(g) * S
+residual = beta * (v - sum_K(S_decay * k))
+S_next = S_decay + residual[..., V, None] * k[..., None, K]
+out = sum_K(S_next * (q * scale))
+```
+
+The default scale (`scale=None`) is `K**-0.5`; explicit scales, including zero,
+are preserved. L2 normalization uses `x / sqrt(sum(x*x) + 1e-6)`, matching
+`gdn/_triton/l2norm.py` and the fused decode recurrence in `gdn/triton.py`,
+**not** `torch.nn.functional.normalize`'s clamp convention. Like upstream
+chunk prefill, prefill rounds normalized Q/K to their input dtype before the
+FP32 recurrence. Decode keeps normalized Q/K in FP32, like upstream fused
+decode. Thus low-precision chunk-vs-step results need not be bitwise equal.
+Outputs always have `q.dtype`; final states always remain FP32. There is no
+BF16-state substitution to call `npu_recurrent_gated_delta_rule`.
+
+## Prefill and checkpoints
+
+Packed prefill takes `[1,T,H,K]` Q/K and `[1,T,HV,V]` values, or their
+unbatched `[T,...]` forms. Log forget and beta gates have matching `[1,T,HV]`
+or `[T,HV]` shapes. `cu_seqlens[N+1]` must be monotone and cover exactly `T`;
+empty sequences retain their initial state. Nonzero initial states are read
+without mutation in the shared K-last `[N,HV,V,K]` ABI. The result is a real
+`GdnChunkPrefillResult` with output, optional final state, `h=None`,
+`h_cu_starts=None`, and `h_layout=NONE`. `output_final_state=False` returns
+`final_state=None` but does not suppress requested checkpoint writes.
+
+`GdnStateCheckpoints` is implemented, so `state_checkpoints=True` is advertised.
+Intervals must be positive multiples of 64, as in the shared contract. After
+each whole interval of a sequence's **own** tokens, the scan copies its actual
+FP32 K-last state to the caller's buffer. `cu_starts` must be int32 on the
+input device and exactly match cumulative per-sequence interval counts; the
+buffer shape must be `[sum(length_i // interval),HV,V,K]`. It cannot alias
+the initial state. Checkpoint positions are relative to the supplied sequence
+start, not an inferred global token count. Allocation, publication and copying
+into LCM state blocks remain runtime responsibilities; no backend-private
+persistent request state is introduced.
+
+Backend-native `output_h` is **unsupported** (`output_h=False` trait only).
+Normal selection rejects it; a forced override raises explicitly. No placeholder
+checkpoints or invented backend layout are returned.
+
+## Decode, destinations and padding
+
+Decode is strictly `T=1`: Q/K `[B,1,H,K]`, V `[B,1,HV,V]` and `a,b` `[B,1,HV]`.
+Decay parameters `A_log,dt_bias[HV]` are converted to FP32 on device. Gates are
+`g = -exp(A_log) * softplus(a + dt_bias, beta=1, threshold=20)` and
+`beta = sigmoid(b)`. Arbitrary input and state strides are supported.
+
+The FP32 pool is `[pool,HV,V,K]`. `initial_state_indices[B]` selects sources;
+`output_state_indices[B]` selects destinations in that same pool, or `None`
+means write to source rows. All sources are snapshotted before any writes,
+so shared input checkpoints and crossing source/destination maps are safe.
+Active destinations must be unique; ambiguous repeated writes are rejected.
+Non-destination rows are unchanged, including shared source rows when separate
+destinations are used. Both index vectors accept int32 or int64.
+
+A negative read index is padding: output is zero and **no pool row is written**,
+even if its destination index is positive. A valid source with a negative
+destination computes its output but suppresses the write. Positive indices
+outside the pool are errors, not clamped. No sacrificial row zero is used.
+There is no separate output-pool argument in the public single-step API;
+distinct source/destination *rows* are supported. MTP, trees and replay APIs
+are not registered by this integration.
+
+## Device and execution limits
+
+All activation and state math stays on the original device, without CPU copies
+or a CPU math fallback. Only integer sequence offsets, checkpoint starts and
+pool indices use `.tolist()`, which **synchronizes NPU metadata to the host**.
+Python loops then enqueue per-token PyTorch arithmetic and copies on the current
+stream. This is intentionally eager and **not graph-capturable**; prefill is not
+in the shared capturable-kernel allowlist. No capture support is advertised.
+Performance on long prompts will be poor, and real-device numerical validation
+is required before deployment.
+
+Direct vendor launchers accept CPU tensors so the same implementation can be
+unit tested without an accelerator. This is explicit CPU execution in tests,
+not a runtime fallback: registry adapters require NPU tensors, and mismatched
+activation/state devices raise instead of transferring tensors.
+
+## Focused verification
+
+With PyTorch installed and the NPU package importable, from the repository root:
+
+```bash
+PYTHONPATH=tokenspeed-kernel-npu/python:$PYTHONPATH \
+  python -m pytest tokenspeed-kernel-npu/test/test_gdn.py -k cpu -q
+
+# On Ascend, with shared kernel dependencies installed as well:
+PYTHONPATH=tokenspeed-kernel-npu/python:tokenspeed-kernel/python:$PYTHONPATH \
+  python -m pytest tokenspeed-kernel-npu/test/test_gdn.py -k npu -q
+```
+
+Direct tests use an independent FP64, K-major, per-head recurrence, exercise
+chunk vs repeated step, nonzero initial states, variable/empty sequences,
+real checkpoint/resume boundaries, low-precision output and normalization,
+grouped heads, arbitrary strides, source nonmutation, crossing maps, negative
+padding and write suppression. Invalid ABI inputs are rejected before writes.
+The NPU-only public test additionally exercises registry selection, structured
+returns, trait queries and explicit `output_h` rejection. Test-only reference
+and result comparisons may transfer tensors to CPU; the implementation does not.
